{"v": 1, "ts": "2026-08-14T00:25:00Z", "actor": "orchestrator", "type": "process_finding", "ref_id": "CHANGE-0143", "finding": "Planning falsified this scope's own intake premise. The intake asserted the close ceremony does not own the regeneration ordering; in fact close-work-item.mjs ALREADY regenerates overview, USER_GUIDE rollup, docs-hub and the factory report (lines 1200-1203) and lost both incidents anyway, because SKILL_PR runs the allocator at step 1b (before staging) while the close ceremony runs at step 5c (after gh pr create) - the stale bytes are committed, pushed and read by the review bots long before the close regen fires. The real seam is the allocator: it rewrites the SOURCES and regenerates only ONE projection (docs/INDEX.md), leaving the USER_GUIDE rollup block and overview holding the dead SPEC-DRAFT path. Verified byte-exactly on the real trees at 00bdd03 (3 files) and ff8208e (2 files).", "decision": "PARKED FOR OPERATOR SIGN-OFF, not proceeded autonomously. The prescribed fix edits .aai/scripts/allocate-doc-number.mjs, which is listed in protected_paths_l3, so Planning correctly raised ceremony 1 -> 3 and the standing engagement rule (full autonomy EXCEPT ceremony-L3 protected surfaces) forbids autopilot from touching it. Recorded as a human-input block in STATE with two options: (A) approve the L3 allocator edit plus the detection test, or (B) detection-only at L1 - a test that fails when a tracked generated page cites a SPEC-DRAFT path whose numbered counterpart exists. Spec 248ccfa stays frozen on branch feat/close-regenerate-order; the chain proceeds with the next scope rather than idling until morning.", "residual": "Until either option lands, the ordering remains human discipline - the exact failure mode that produced the defect twice in one day.", "source": "docs/specs/SPEC-DRAFT-spec-close-regenerate-order.md; Planning report 2026-08-14T00:22Z"}
